			"notes": "# The Dependency Update\n\n*So, uhh.... This is kinda awkward!* Turns out that KTor isn't a part of Kotlin itself, at least not of the probably-somewhat-cut-down Kotlin distribution that Fabric Kotlin ships (I mean... I bet they weren't expecting anyone to build a webserver inside a mod), so it has to be included in the JAR by me. I wasn't doing that. Effect? Both `v0.1` and `v0.2` are actually broken and will throw a `ClassNotFoundException` when you try to open a world (outside a dev environment (where all Gradle dependencies are always added), which is why I didn't notice up until now). *Whoops.* Anyway, **this is the first update that actually works**, yaayyyyy! Also, fixes two very minor issues (a typo (an extra `.` in a log message) and the fact that the terrain wasn't loading if you didn't have an extra Surveyor frontend (eg. Hoofprint/AA4) or a Surveyor config flag set).\n\nAlso published on [Modrinth](https://modrinth.com/mod/surwebyor/version/v0.3).\n\n*Note: It's marked as „pre-release” due to several outstanding problems, all listed [here](https://github.com/GuzioMG/Surwebyor/blob/9c9caf0c6fa12ca8af2efb1cbadb17f9cb9181aa/README.md#warning-early-alpha-alert). However, it is USABLE to enough of an extent (the terrain is shown as it's supposed to, and critical config options needed for deployment in the real world are all available) that I feel safe showing it, in a pinch. And in a pinch I am, because I wanted to get in the Modrinth review queue as soon as possible. Which, uhh... May have been a bad idea, actually. Because it was this rushing that brought me to this place, where 2 releases in a row are broken, in the first place.*"
		},
